# Spring Security 5 to 6 Migration Guide

## Overview

Spring Security 6 (used in Spring Boot 3.x) removes many deprecated APIs from Spring Security 5. The biggest change is the removal of `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter` and migration to component-based security configuration.

## Key Changes

### 1. Remove WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter

The `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter` class is removed. Security configuration must use `SecurityFilterChain` bean.

```java
// BEFORE (Spring Security 5 / Spring Boot 2.x)
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true)
public class SecurityConfig extends WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {

    @Autowired
    private UserDetailsService userDetailsService;

    @Override
    protected void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .csrf().disable()
            .sessionManagement()
                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS)
            .and()
            .authorizeRequests()
                .antMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/api/users").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated();
    }

    @Override
    protected void configure(AuthenticationManagerBuilder auth) throws Exception {
        auth.userDetailsService(userDetailsService)
            .passwordEncoder(passwordEncoder());
    }

    @Override
    @Bean
    public AuthenticationManager authenticationManagerBean() throws Exception {
        return super.authenticationManagerBean();
    }
}

// AFTER (Spring Security 6 / Spring Boot 3.x)
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableMethodSecurity  // replaces @EnableGlobalMethodSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
            .sessionManagement(session ->
                session.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/api/users").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated());
        return http.build();
    }

    @Bean
    public AuthenticationManager authenticationManager(
            AuthenticationConfiguration authenticationConfiguration) throws Exception {
        return authenticationConfiguration.getAuthenticationManager();
    }

    @Bean
    public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
        return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
    }
}
```

### 2. Method Security Annotation Change

```java
// Before
@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true)

// After
@EnableMethodSecurity  // prePostEnabled is true by default
```

### 3. URL Matcher Changes

```java
// Before - antMatchers()
.antMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/api/users").permitAll()
.antMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll()

// After - requestMatchers()
.requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/api/users").permitAll()
.requestMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll()
```

### 4. Lambda DSL (Required in Spring Security 6)

The `.and()` chaining style is deprecated/removed. Use lambda DSL:

```java
// Before (chaining with .and())
http
    .csrf().disable()
    .sessionManagement()
        .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS)
    .and()
    .exceptionHandling()
        .authenticationEntryPoint(...)
    .and()
    .authorizeRequests()
        .antMatchers("/public").permitAll()
        .anyRequest().authenticated()
    .and()
    .headers().frameOptions().disable();

// After (lambda DSL)
http
    .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
    .sessionManagement(session ->
        session.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
    .exceptionHandling(ex ->
        ex.authenticationEntryPoint(...))
    .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
        .requestMatchers("/public").permitAll()
        .anyRequest().authenticated())
    .headers(headers ->
        headers.frameOptions(frame -> frame.disable()));
```

### 5. authorizeRequests() → authorizeHttpRequests()

```java
// Before
.authorizeRequests()

// After
.authorizeHttpRequests()
```

### 6. AuthenticationManager Bean

```java
// Before - override method
@Override
@Bean
public AuthenticationManager authenticationManagerBean() throws Exception {
    return super.authenticationManagerBean();
}

// After - use AuthenticationConfiguration
@Bean
public AuthenticationManager authenticationManager(
        AuthenticationConfiguration config) throws Exception {
    return config.getAuthenticationManager();
}
```

### 7. UserDetailsService Configuration

In Spring Security 6 with Spring Boot 3, if you define a `UserDetailsService` bean and a `PasswordEncoder` bean, Spring Boot auto-configures the `DaoAuthenticationProvider`. No need to manually configure `AuthenticationManagerBuilder`.

## Import Changes

```java
// Removed
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.configuration.WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.method.configuration.EnableGlobalMethodSecurity;

// Added
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.method.configuration.EnableMethodSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.authentication.configuration.AuthenticationConfiguration;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;
```

# Hibernate 5 to Hibernate 6 Migration Guide

## Overview

Spring Boot 3.x uses Hibernate 6 (specifically Hibernate ORM 6.x), which includes several breaking changes from Hibernate 5.x used in Spring Boot 2.x.

## Key Changes

### 1. Dialect Configuration

Hibernate 6 auto-detects the dialect in most cases. The explicit dialect configuration can be simplified or removed.

```properties
# Before (Spring Boot 2.x / Hibernate 5)
spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.H2Dialect

# After (Spring Boot 3.x / Hibernate 6)
# Option 1: Remove entirely - Hibernate 6 auto-detects
# Option 2: Keep it - the class name is the same for H2
spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.H2Dialect
```

Note: For H2, the dialect class name remains `org.hibernate.dialect.H2Dialect` in Hibernate 6, but it's in a different package hierarchy internally. Leaving the property works fine.

### 2. ID Generation Strategy Changes

Hibernate 6 changes the default ID generation strategy. The `GenerationType.IDENTITY` strategy still works the same way, but `GenerationType.AUTO` behavior has changed:

```java
// IDENTITY strategy - works the same in both versions
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
private Long id;

// AUTO strategy - behavior changed in Hibernate 6
// In Hibernate 5: defaults to IDENTITY for most databases
// In Hibernate 6: uses sequence-based generation by default
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.AUTO)
private Long id;
```

If using `GenerationType.IDENTITY`, no changes needed.

### 3. Namespace Changes (JPA)

All `javax.persistence` imports must change to `jakarta.persistence`:

```java
// Before
import javax.persistence.Entity;
import javax.persistence.Table;
import javax.persistence.Id;
import javax.persistence.GeneratedValue;
import javax.persistence.GenerationType;
import javax.persistence.Column;
import javax.persistence.Enumerated;
import javax.persistence.EnumType;
import javax.persistence.PrePersist;
import javax.persistence.PreUpdate;
import javax.persistence.EntityNotFoundException;

// After
import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.Table;
import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import jakarta.persistence.GeneratedValue;
import jakarta.persistence.GenerationType;
import jakarta.persistence.Column;
import jakarta.persistence.Enumerated;
import jakarta.persistence.EnumType;
import jakarta.persistence.PrePersist;
import jakarta.persistence.PreUpdate;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityNotFoundException;
```

### 4. JPQL Changes

Hibernate 6 is stricter about JPQL queries. Some implicit behaviors are now explicit:

```java
// These common patterns still work in Hibernate 6:
@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.active = true AND u.role = :role")
List<User> findActiveUsersByRole(@Param("role") Role role);

@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE LOWER(u.username) LIKE LOWER(CONCAT('%', :searchTerm, '%'))")
List<User> searchUsers(@Param("searchTerm") String searchTerm);
```

### 5. Type System Changes

Hibernate 6 has a completely rewritten type system. For most standard JPA usage (entities, basic types, enums), no code changes are needed. Custom Hibernate types may need migration.

### 6. Spring Data JPA Repository

Spring Data JPA repositories remain compatible. No changes needed for standard CRUD repositories:

```java
@Repository
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    Optional<User> findByUsername(String username);
    boolean existsByUsername(String username);
    List<User> findByRole(Role role);
    List<User> findByActiveTrue();
}
```

## Application Properties Changes

```properties
# These properties work the same in Spring Boot 3.x / Hibernate 6
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop
spring.jpa.show-sql=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.format_sql=true
```

## Summary

For a standard JPA application (entities with basic types, enums, standard repositories), the Hibernate 6 migration primarily involves:
1. `javax.persistence` → `jakarta.persistence` namespace changes
2. Verifying ID generation strategy (IDENTITY is fine, AUTO may need attention)
3. No changes needed for standard JPQL queries and Spring Data JPA repositories

# RestTemplate to RestClient Migration Guide

## Overview

Spring Boot 3.2 introduces `RestClient` as the modern, fluent replacement for `RestTemplate`. `RestClient` provides a synchronous HTTP client with a fluent API similar to `WebClient` but without requiring reactive dependencies.

## Key Differences

| Feature | RestTemplate | RestClient |
|---|---|---|
| API Style | Template methods | Fluent builder |
| Introduced | Spring 3.0 | Spring 6.1 / Boot 3.2 |
| Status | Maintenance mode | Actively developed |
| Configuration | Constructor + interceptors | Builder pattern |

## Migration Patterns

### 1. Creating the Client

```java
// Before (RestTemplate)
private final RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();

// After (RestClient)
private final RestClient restClient = RestClient.create();

// Or with base URL
private final RestClient restClient = RestClient.builder()
    .baseUrl("https://api.example.com")
    .build();
```

### 2. GET Request

```java
// Before (RestTemplate)
ResponseEntity<Map> response = restTemplate.getForEntity(url, Map.class);
Map body = response.getBody();

// After (RestClient)
Map body = restClient.get()
    .uri(url)
    .retrieve()
    .body(Map.class);

// Or if you need the full ResponseEntity
ResponseEntity<Map> response = restClient.get()
    .uri(url)
    .retrieve()
    .toEntity(Map.class);
```

### 3. POST Request

```java
// Before (RestTemplate)
HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON);
HttpEntity<Map<String, String>> request = new HttpEntity<>(payload, headers);
restTemplate.postForEntity(url, request, Void.class);

// After (RestClient)
restClient.post()
    .uri(url)
    .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
    .body(payload)
    .retrieve()
    .toBodilessEntity();
```

### 4. Exchange (GET with Headers)

```java
// Before (RestTemplate)
HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.setAccept(Collections.singletonList(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON));
HttpEntity<?> request = new HttpEntity<>(headers);
ResponseEntity<Map> response = restTemplate.exchange(
    url, HttpMethod.GET, request, Map.class
);

// After (RestClient)
Map body = restClient.get()
    .uri(url)
    .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
    .retrieve()
    .body(Map.class);
```

### 5. DELETE Request

```java
// Before (RestTemplate)
restTemplate.delete(url);

// After (RestClient)
restClient.delete()
    .uri(url)
    .retrieve()
    .toBodilessEntity();
```

## Complete Migration Example

```java
// BEFORE: ExternalApiService with RestTemplate
@Service
public class ExternalApiService {
    private final RestTemplate restTemplate;

    @Value("${external.api.base-url}")
    private String baseUrl;

    public ExternalApiService() {
        this.restTemplate = new RestTemplate();
    }

    public boolean verifyEmail(String email) {
        try {
            String url = baseUrl + "/verify/email?email=" + email;
            ResponseEntity<Map> response = restTemplate.getForEntity(url, Map.class);
            if (response.getBody() != null) {
                return Boolean.TRUE.equals(response.getBody().get("valid"));
            }
            return false;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public void sendNotification(String userId, String message) {
        try {
            String url = baseUrl + "/notifications";
            HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
            headers.setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON);
            Map<String, String> payload = Map.of(
                "userId", userId, "message", message, "type", "USER_UPDATE"
            );
            HttpEntity<Map<String, String>> request = new HttpEntity<>(payload, headers);
            restTemplate.postForEntity(url, request, Void.class);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.err.println("Failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

// AFTER: ExternalApiService with RestClient
@Service
public class ExternalApiService {
    private final RestClient restClient;

    public ExternalApiService(@Value("${external.api.base-url:https://api.example.com}") String baseUrl) {
        this.restClient = RestClient.builder()
            .baseUrl(baseUrl)
            .build();
    }

    public boolean verifyEmail(String email) {
        try {
            Map body = restClient.get()
                .uri("/verify/email?email=" + email)
                .retrieve()
                .body(Map.class);
            if (body != null) {
                return Boolean.TRUE.equals(body.get("valid"));
            }
            return false;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public void sendNotification(String userId, String message) {
        try {
            Map<String, String> payload = Map.of(
                "userId", userId, "message", message, "type", "USER_UPDATE"
            );
            restClient.post()
                .uri("/notifications")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .body(payload)
                .retrieve()
                .toBodilessEntity();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.err.println("Failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

## Important Notes

- `RestClient` is synchronous (blocking), just like `RestTemplate`
- No additional dependencies needed — it's part of `spring-boot-starter-web`
- `RestClient.create()` creates a basic instance; `RestClient.builder()` allows customization
- The `retrieve()` method handles non-2xx responses by throwing exceptions by default
- Use `body(Class)` to get just the body, or `toEntity(Class)` for the full `ResponseEntity`
- Use `toBodilessEntity()` when no response body is expected

